 {"q": "How should I choose between RAG and fine-tuning?",
  "a": "Ask three questions. Does the knowledge change? If yes, RAG — weights shouldn't chase moving facts. Do answers need citations? If yes, RAG — grounding is traceable. Do you need behavior or style internalized (tone, format, reasoning patterns)? That's fine-tuning's job. Then run both on the same task and measure: retrieval hit-rate and groundedness for RAG, task performance plus forgetting checks for the tuned model. The experiment decides; the heuristics just pick the starting lineup."},
 {"q": "My agent works in demos but loops and drifts in real tasks. What's the fix?",
  "a": "Treat the loop as the system, not the model. Add a step budget with a loop breaker (progress detection, escalate-to-human), restate the goal with checkpoint summaries to fight drift, and bound tool capabilities with least privilege plus human gates on irreversible actions. Then instrument: log every iteration (thought, action, observation) and meter per-run cost. Most 'agent failures' are missing control flow, not missing intelligence — the fixes are engineering, and they're all in the agentic path's failure catalog."},
 {"q": "How do I know my evaluation numbers are honest?",
  "a": "Run the checklist: test data untouched during development (no peeking), preprocessing inside the pipeline inside the fold (no leakage), metrics matched to the business cost of each error type (no accuracy-on-imbalance lies), intervals around the headline number (no naked point estimates), and ablations showing what actually helped. Then slice the results — the worst slice is usually the most informative. If any check fails, the number is a claim, not a result."},
